course: dictionary
lesson: 8733
title: Words from history
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Serfdom is a history word. It names a system in the Middle Ages where poor farm workers, called serfs, were tied to a lord's land and could not leave it. Context gives you clues: serfs were farm workers, and "tied to" and "could not leave" show they were not free.
explain: Look at «Taras Shevchenko was born a serf, was bought out of serfdom in 1838». The words "bought out" tell you serfdom is a state a person could be freed from. Try your meaning in the sentence: "bought out of the state of being a serf" still makes sense, so you have got it.
explain: Other phrases help too. «The end of serfdom» and «abolish serfdom» show that serfdom could be ended by people or laws. So serfdom was a system that changed over time, not something that always stayed the same.
fact: Serfdom means the state of being a serf.
fact: A serf was a poor farm worker, tied to a lord's land, who could not leave it freely.
fact: Serfdom is mainly linked to the Middle Ages and the feudal system.
fact: «Serfdom ended in the Austrian Empire in 1848 and in the Russian Empire in 1861.»
fact: Serfdom is an uncountable noun, so we say «the end of serfdom», not «serfdoms».
exercise: «The century also brought the end of serfdom.» What does serfdom mean here? | A new kind of port | A kind of coal and steel | Writing poems and books | *The state of being a serf, tied to a lord's land
exercise: «Serfs were farmers tied to a landlord's land who could not leave freely.» Which word shows that serfs were held to the land? | freely | landlord's | farmers | *tied
exercise: Why was serfdom not exactly slavery? | Serfs could move to any city they liked | Serfs were paid a lot of money | *Serfs could not be sold apart from the land | Serfs were the sons of kings
test: What does serfdom mean? | A kind of coal mine | A new port by the sea | Being a king or a lord | *The state of being a serf
test: «Orthodox peasants later faced heavier serfdom.» What does heavier serfdom mean? | *A harder tie to a landowner's land and more work for him | Lighter work and more free time | Living freely in a city | A holiday from farming
exam: «Serfdom ended in the Austrian Empire in 1848.» What does the word ended tell you about serfdom? | *It came to an end | It went on forever | It moved to the city | It grew bigger
